Now that's the Web part now we have mobile Users to cater with same So there is another Pattern introduced Calles

### BFF Pattern - Backend For Frontend

---- In this we split our backend so each frontend application get it's individual backend so they don't share the same
because mobile application has different views and has different shape of data then a desktop application.

That when GraphQL comes to solve the over fetching or under fetching problem on RESP API's
--// PRO's
Tailored endpoints, Faster interation, Aggregates services
--// Cons
Tight coupling, Scalability limits(still higher than SPA's), Single point of failure

# SSG/ISG/SSR -----------------//

1. SSG - Static Site Generation - (simple site generation - generate on backend then rendered on frontend) {1}
2. ISR - Incremental Static Regenration (Upgrade over SSG ) - {2}
   -- In this you basically regenerate your page based in TTL (Time to Live)
   if users make a request and it's over certain time window then you can go back to your server/storage, regenerate that then place it in the cache in CDN
   Better than SSG still not good enough to build anything interactive

(---- So Next Approach -----)

3. SSR - Server Side Rendering - {3}
   -- In this the Key Concept is "Hyderation"
   -- it still works the same concept render on backend and send the prerendered page to frontend but to achieve interactivity we need hyderations and Hyderations lifecycle like this :
   -- Server Render the page and create a HTML string then
   -- Initial Paing - Browser renders that inital HTML immediately giving the user Fast Contentful Paint(FCP) with DRY (non-interactive page)
   -- Download & Execute - These Client side download the javascript bundle and execute it
   -- Hyderation - the Framework traverse the DOM and attached to the prerendered HTML to make it interactive (like binding event handlers etc) and all of this is handle by React or framework

" But it still takes a long time until the user's can actually interact with webpage and click on something and nothing will happen
and to achieve more faster interactive webpage there is tool like RSC "

4. RSC - React Server Components {4}
   -- Splits the tree, Some components render only on the server.
   Smaller client bundles, less hyderation work.
   Faster interactivity, but higher mental/model complexity.

(----- New Way To Handle It -------------)

### Modular Frontend Monolith - (Split application into multiple domains)

"Apps is too Big to Handle"
In this you add boundaries between modules
(Billing module, Product Module, Payment Module, Users etc)
(Shared UI & Logics, Shared Services)

--// Pro - Cohesion, Global State
--// Con - Long build time, Hard to enforce boundaries

---- Software Architecture ----
Classical Architecture - types of architecture are :

1. The Clean Architecture (way optmized for backend) - (not so common)
   -- you can change the frontend part of thing withc ot needing to change the backend or structure,
   -- basically you can swap out whole frontend framework and you wouldn't have to change the whole thing

2. Hexagonal Architecture (more focused on how you connect external dependencies into your domain)
   -- also called onion architecture
   basically at the center is whole business logic and outer is external dependencies

### Microfrontends - (SPA meets enterprise software)

-- In this we litteraly take those domains and deploy them as independent applications and put them together at runtime.
for ex. like Header, Footer those are independent application and there is shell component that put them together.
And that SHELL has some shared components, CSS or shared state that will inject in those applications.
-- Highly scalable in term of developement speed where lot of developer contributing on it.
-- Best fit for large organizations with independent teams

### Architecture Audit

---

---

## SYSTEM DESIGN

---

"How would you design a frontend architecture that supports multiple products with shared UI components, reusable business logic, and centralized authentication?"

Follow up Questions:

## Product / application scope

"How many products are we supporting?"
"Are these separate applications or different modules of the same application?"
"Do they have separate deployment cycles?"
"Are they built using the same framework, for example React/Next.js?"
"Do different products have different teams?"

## Shared code

"How much UI is expected to be shared?"
"Are business rules also shared, or mainly UI components?"
"Do products need to customize shared components?"

## Authentication

"Is authentication centralized through something like OAuth/OIDC?"
"Do all products share the same user/session?"
"Are permissions/roles different between products?"

## Scale

"Approximately how many developers and products are we talking about?"
"Do we expect third-party teams to consume the shared components?"
Then explain your approach

You could say:

"I'd first separate the problem into three concerns: product applications, shared UI/design-system code, and shared business/authentication logic."

Then propose something like:

                    ┌─────────────────────┐
                    │ Identity / Auth     │
                    │ OAuth / OIDC / SSO  │
                    └──────────┬──────────┘
                               │
          ┌────────────────────┼────────────────────┐
          │                    │                    │
     Product A             Product B            Product C
          │                    │                    │
          └──────────────┬─────┴─────┬──────────────┘
                         │           │
                  ┌──────▼──────┐ ┌──▼────────────┐
                  │ Design      │ │ Shared        │
                  │ System      │ │ Business      │
                  │ Components  │ │ Logic         │
                  └─────────────┘ └───────────────┘

For example:

packages/
ui/
design-tokens/
auth/
api-client/
business-logic/
utilities/

apps/
product-a/
product-b/
product-c/
Important follow-up

Explain what you would NOT share.

For example:

"I wouldn't try to put every piece of code into a shared package. Product-specific workflows and domain logic should remain inside the product. Otherwise the shared package becomes a tightly coupled monolith."

That's a strong architectural point.

If they ask "Why?"

Talk about:

consistency
maintainability
independent product development
reduced duplication
versioning
dependency management
avoiding excessive coupling

