<!-- ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- -->

1. useTransition() - The useTransition hook is a React concurrent feature that lets you update the state without blocking the user interface. It allows you to split your state updates into two categories: urgent updates (like typing in an input) which render immediately, and transitions (like filtering a massive list) which are processed in the background and can be interrupted if the user does something else

it also replace the separate loading state like "isLoading" logic with it "isPending" (its a boolean value) when component load the data

~ const [isPending, startTransition] = useTransition()
const switchTabs = (tab) => startTransition(() => setTab(tab))

inside jsx -> {isPending && <p>Content is loading</p>}

2. React Compiler - so React.memo(), useMemo or useCallback become obsolete

3. FormAction = it eliminate the code to handle form data all inputs controlled react and also don't need to include preventDefault function

4. useActionState() -> const [state, actionFunction, isPending] = useActionState(fn, initial state)

fn - action or reducerAction is want to run when the form gets submitted or whenever the actionFunction is gonna run
actionFunctoin - <form action={actionFunction}>
isPending - same loading state

The Form snippet using useActioState :
{
const [name, actionFunction, isPending] = useActionState(updateName, {
error: null,
name: JSON.parse(localStorage.getItem("name")) || "Anonymous User"
})
async function updateName(prevState, formData){

try{
const newName = await updateName(formData.get ("name")) [p.s (input field value name = "value")]
return newName
} catch(error){
return {...prevState, error}
}
}

return(
<> <p>{name}</p>
{isPending && <p>Loading Data</p>}

   <form action={actionFunction}>
   <input type="text" name="name" required />
   <button type="submit "> Submit</button>
   </form>
   {state.error && <p>{state.error.message}</p>}
   </>
)
}

5. useOptimistic() - provides optimistic state that can update to user immediately and will be automatically reverted if there's a problem with the update to the server.

const [optimisticName, setOptimisticName] = useOptimistic(state.name)

6. useFormStatus() - act as a context consumer for the nearest parent element and provides you information about the current status of that form.
   in another words a Hook that gives you status information of the last form submission.

7. use refs as a props Now we have dedicated "ref" prop
   previously there is workaround be forwardRef function and then we have to pass ref to that component

8. use() - Not a hook it's a API

- Reads async resources and automatically suspends the component.
- can replace the useContext hook

## Before use() example fetching data from API

const [pokemon, setPokemon] = useState(null)

useEffect(() => {
fetch(" Api------URL")
.then(res => res.json())
.then(data => setPokemon(data))
}, [])

return(
<>
<code>{data result} </code>
</>
)

## after use() example fetching data from API

const fetchPromise = fetch("URL ----------- API")
.then(res => res.json())

const pokemon = use(fetchPromise)

rest stays same.....
